module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Limits
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Noetherian
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.CohenMacaulaySlice.OpenImmersionRegular
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.PerfectMorphism.RegularImmersion
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.RegularIdealSheaf.FlatBaseChange
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.RelativeRegularImmersion.SyntomicIntoSmooth
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Misc.SpecProductRing
public import StacksFrontier.Examples.Scheme.RegularImmersionNotPermanentInMiddle
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# The outer factor of a perfect factorization need not be a regular immersion

Stacks tag 09RK (Lemma 37.61.15) reads: *let `i : Z → Y` and `j : Y → X` be immersions of
schemes; assume (1) `X` is locally Noetherian, (2) `j ∘ i` is a regular immersion, and (3) `i` is
perfect; then `i` and `j` are regular immersions.*  The conclusion about `j` is false as written,
and this file refutes it.  This is a **Stacks erratum**.

The counterexample is a disjoint union, realized here as a coproduct of two affine schemes.  Let
`k` be a field, write `Z = Spec k` and put

* `X = Spec k ⊔ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`,
* `Y = Spec k ⊔ Spec k`,
* `j : Y ⟶ X` the identity on the first component and the reduced point `Spec k ↪ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`
  on the second,
* `i : Z ⟶ Y` the inclusion of the first component.

Then `X` is locally Noetherian, being the spectrum of the Noetherian ring
`k × k[ε]/(ε²)`.  Both `i` and `j ∘ i` are the inclusion of a connected component, hence open
immersions, hence regular immersions; so `i` is perfect and hypothesis (2) holds.  But `j` is not
a regular immersion: its restriction to the second component is the reduced point of the double
point, whose ideal `(ε) ⊆ k[ε]/(ε²)` is generated by no weakly regular sequence, every element of
it being a zerodivisor.

The false step in the source is the inference from regularity of `i` and of `j ∘ i` to regularity
of `j`.  The source's argument is local at the points of `i(Z)` — it produces, for each `z : Z`, a
regular sequence generating the kernel of `O_{X, j (i z)} ⟶ O_{Y, i z}` — and says nothing at
points of `Y` outside `i(Z)`.  Here `i(Z)` is the first component, and it is precisely on the
second component that `j` fails to be regular.  The corrected statement, which is what the
source's own argument gives, is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.exists_opens_isRegularImmersion_comp_of_perfect`:
there is an open `V ⊆ Y` containing `i(Z)` with `V.ι ≫ j` a regular immersion.  Both that
declaration and the refutation below carry the Stacks tag 09RK, the latter as the erratum's
counterexample.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_isRegularImmersion_of_perfect_of_isRegularImmersion_comp`:
  **the refutation of Stacks tag 09RK as stated**, the negation of the tag's own universally
  quantified assertion.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersion_erratumOuter`: `j` is not a regular immersion.
* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_erratumInner`,
  `Stacks.Examples.perfect_erratumInner`,
  `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_erratumInner_comp`,
  `Stacks.Examples.isLocallyNoetherian_erratumAmbient`: the hypotheses of the tag, verified for
  this `i` and `j`.  Only the second half of the tag's conclusion fails; `i` really is a regular
  immersion.
* `Stacks.Examples.isRegularImmersion_of_isOpenImmersion`: an open immersion of schemes is a
  regular immersion.  This is the regular case of what the library already had for the
  Koszul-regular, `H₁`-regular and quasi-regular conditions, and it is general, not about the
  example.

## Implementation notes

`Spec k` is spelled `Spec (k[x]/(x))` throughout, as `Stacks.Examples.SimpleOriginRing`, and
`Spec k[ε]/(ε²)` is spelled `Spec (k[x]/(x²))`, as `Stacks.Examples.DoubleOriginRing`.  This is
so that the second component of `j` is literally `Stacks.Examples.simpleOriginImmersion`, the
reduced point of the double point, for which
`Stacks.Examples.not_isRegularImmersion_simpleOriginImmersion` already refutes regularity: that
is the whole arithmetic content of the counterexample, and it is not reproved here.

The disjoint union is the categorical coproduct of schemes rather than `Spec` of a product ring,
because the coproduct makes the two positive halves free: `i` and `j ∘ i` are both
`CategoryTheory.Limits.coprod.inl`, by `CategoryTheory.Limits.coprod.inl_map`, and the inclusions
of a coproduct are open immersions.  The product ring is still what supplies local
Noetherianness, through `AlgebraicGeometry.coprodSpec`, which identifies the coproduct with
`Spec (k[x]/(x) × k[x]/(x²))`.

Regularity descends from `j` to its restriction to the second component because that restriction
sits in a cartesian square over `j` whose base change is the open immersion of the component,
which is flat;  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_isPullback_of_flat` (Stacks tag
067P) then applies.  The square is cartesian because `Scheme` is finitary extensive, so its
binary coproducts are van Kampen colimits — this is the step that would otherwise have to be
done by hand on sections or on local rings.

Which of the four conditions of Stacks tag 063J is refuted does not matter: `X` is locally
Noetherian, so they agree by Stacks tag 063L.  The one used is part (1),
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.IsRegularImmersion`, the condition the source's
statement is about.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 37.61.15 (Tag 09RK)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09RK)
* [Stacks Project, Definition 37.61.2 (Tag 0687)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0687)
* [Stacks Project, Definition 31.21.4 (Tag 063J)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063J)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 31.21.5 (Tag 063L)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063L)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 31.20.4 (Tag 067P)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/067P)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CommRingCat
open AlgebraicGeometry
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf

namespace Stacks.Examples

universe u

variable (k : Type u) [Field k]

/-! ### Two general statements -/

/-- **An open immersion of schemes is a regular immersion.**  It is an immersion between two
schemes smooth over its target, so Stacks tag 067U applies.  The library already had this for
parts (2), (3) and (4) of Stacks tag 063J; this is part (1). -/
@[ega "IV 19.1.5" "(i)"]
theorem isRegularImmersion_of_isOpenImmersion {A B : Scheme.{u}} (e : A ⟶ B)
    [IsOpenImmersion e] : IsRegularImmersion e :=
  Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_smooth_of_smooth e (𝟙 B)

/-- Regularity transports along an equality of immersions.  The instance argument of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.IsRegularImmersion` makes the corresponding rewrite
fail on the motive. -/
theorem isRegularImmersion_of_eq {A B : Scheme.{u}} {a b : A ⟶ B} [IsImmersion a] [IsImmersion b]
    (h : a = b) (ha : IsRegularImmersion a) : IsRegularImmersion b := by
  subst h; exact ha

/-! ### The counterexample -/

/-- The reduced point `Z = Spec k`, spelled `Spec (k[x]/(x))`. -/
abbrev reducedPointScheme : Scheme.{u} := Spec (CommRingCat.of (SimpleOriginRing k))

/-- The double point `Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`, spelled `Spec (k[x]/(x²))`. -/
abbrev doublePointScheme : Scheme.{u} := Spec (CommRingCat.of (DoubleOriginRing k))

/-- **The ambient scheme `X = Spec k ⊔ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`** of the counterexample to Stacks tag
09RK. -/
abbrev erratumAmbient : Scheme.{u} := reducedPointScheme k ⨿ doublePointScheme k

/-- **The middle scheme `Y = Spec k ⊔ Spec k`** of the counterexample to Stacks tag 09RK. -/
abbrev erratumMiddle : Scheme.{u} := reducedPointScheme k ⨿ reducedPointScheme k

/-- **The inner immersion `i : Z ⟶ Y`**, the inclusion of the first component of
`Y = Spec k ⊔ Spec k`. -/
abbrev erratumInner : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k := coprod.inl

/-- **The outer immersion `j : Y ⟶ X`**: the identity on the first component, and the reduced
point `Spec k ↪ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)` on the second. -/
def erratumOuter : erratumMiddle k ⟶ erratumAmbient k :=
  coprod.map (𝟙 (reducedPointScheme k)) (simpleOriginImmersion k)

/-- `j ∘ i` is the inclusion of the first component of `X`, because `j` is the identity there. -/
theorem erratumInner_comp_erratumOuter :
    erratumInner k ≫ erratumOuter k = (coprod.inl : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k) := by
  simp [erratumOuter, erratumInner]

/-- On the second component `j` is the reduced point of the double point. -/
theorem coprod_inr_comp_erratumOuter :
    (coprod.inr : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k) ≫ erratumOuter k =
      simpleOriginImmersion k ≫ (coprod.inr : doublePointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k) := by
  simp [erratumOuter]

/-- `j` is a closed immersion, hence an immersion: being a closed immersion is Zariski-local at
the target, and both components of `j` are closed immersions. -/
instance isClosedImmersion_erratumOuter : IsClosedImmersion (erratumOuter k) := by
  refine IsZariskiLocalAtTarget.coprodMap _ _ ?_ inferInstance
  infer_instance

/-- **Hypothesis (1) of Stacks tag 09RK: `X` is locally Noetherian.**  `X` is the coproduct of
two affine schemes, and `AlgebraicGeometry.coprodSpec` identifies it with the spectrum of
`k[x]/(x) × k[x]/(x²)`, a Noetherian ring. -/
instance isLocallyNoetherian_erratumAmbient : IsLocallyNoetherian (erratumAmbient k) :=
  isLocallyNoetherian_of_isOpenImmersion
    (coprodSpec (SimpleOriginRing k) (DoubleOriginRing k))

/-- **`i` is a regular immersion**, being the inclusion of a component, hence an open
immersion.  So the first half of the conclusion of Stacks tag 09RK does hold here: it is only
the half about `j` that fails. -/
theorem isRegularImmersion_erratumInner : IsRegularImmersion (erratumInner k) :=
  isRegularImmersion_of_isOpenImmersion _

/-- **Hypothesis (3) of Stacks tag 09RK: `i` is perfect.**  A regular immersion is perfect, by
Stacks tag 068C. -/
theorem perfect_erratumInner : AlgebraicGeometry.Perfect (erratumInner k) :=
  perfect_of_isRegularImmersion _ (isRegularImmersion_erratumInner k)

/-- **Hypothesis (2) of Stacks tag 09RK: `j ∘ i` is a regular immersion.**  It is the inclusion
of a component of `X`, hence an open immersion. -/
theorem isRegularImmersion_erratumInner_comp :
    IsRegularImmersion (erratumInner k ≫ erratumOuter k) :=
  isRegularImmersion_of_eq (erratumInner_comp_erratumOuter k).symm
    (isRegularImmersion_of_isOpenImmersion _)

/-- The second component of `j` sits in a cartesian square over `j`, the base change being the
inclusion of the second component of `X`.  Binary coproducts of schemes are van Kampen colimits,
`Scheme` being finitary extensive, which is exactly this statement. -/
theorem isPullback_simpleOriginImmersion_erratumOuter :
    IsPullback (simpleOriginImmersion k)
      (coprod.inr : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k)
      (coprod.inr : doublePointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k) (erratumOuter k) := by
  have hvk : IsVanKampenColimit
      (BinaryCofan.mk (coprod.inl : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k)
        (coprod.inr : doublePointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k)) :=
    FinitaryExtensive.vanKampen _ (coprodIsCoprod _ _)
  have h := (BinaryCofan.isVanKampen_iff _).mp hvk
    (BinaryCofan.mk (coprod.inl : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k)
      (coprod.inr : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k))
    (𝟙 (reducedPointScheme k)) (simpleOriginImmersion k) (erratumOuter k)
    (coprod.inl_map _ _).symm (coprod.inr_map _ _).symm
  exact ((h.mp ⟨coprodIsCoprod _ _⟩).2).flip

/-- **`j` is not a regular immersion.**  Its restriction to the second component of `X` is the
reduced point `Spec k ↪ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`, which is not a regular immersion because the ideal
`(ε) ⊆ k[ε]/(ε²)` consists of zerodivisors.  Regularity passes from `j` to that restriction by
flat base change along the inclusion of the component, Stacks tag 067P. -/
theorem not_isRegularImmersion_erratumOuter : ¬ IsRegularImmersion (erratumOuter k) := by
  intro h
  refine not_isRegularImmersion_simpleOriginImmersion k ?_
  exact isRegularImmersion_of_isPullback_of_flat
    (i := erratumOuter k) (j := simpleOriginImmersion k)
    (k := (coprod.inr : reducedPointScheme k ⟶ erratumMiddle k))
    (g := (coprod.inr : doublePointScheme k ⟶ erratumAmbient k))
    (isPullback_simpleOriginImmersion_erratumOuter k) h

include k in
/-- **Stacks tag 09RK is false as stated: a Stacks erratum.**  The
tag asserts that for immersions `i : Z ⟶ Y` and `j : Y ⟶ X` of schemes with `X` locally
Noetherian, `i` perfect and `j ∘ i` a regular immersion, both `i` and `j` are regular immersions.
This is the negation of that assertion.

The witness is `Z = Spec k`, `Y = Spec k ⊔ Spec k`, `X = Spec k ⊔ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)`, with
`i = Stacks.Examples.erratumInner k` the inclusion of the first component of `Y` and
`j = Stacks.Examples.erratumOuter k` the identity on the first component and the reduced point
`Spec k ↪ Spec k[ε]/(ε²)` on the second.  All three hypotheses hold: `X` is the spectrum of the
Noetherian ring `k × k[ε]/(ε²)`, and `i` and `j ∘ i` are both the inclusion of a component,
hence open immersions, hence regular immersions, so `i` is perfect.  The conclusion fails for `j`
on the second component, where its ideal `(ε) ⊆ k[ε]/(ε²)` is generated by no weakly regular
sequence: every element of `(ε)` kills `ε ≠ 0`.

The source's proof establishes regularity of `j` only at the points of `i(Z)` — here the first
component, where `j` is an isomorphism — and the statement it does support is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.exists_opens_isRegularImmersion_comp_of_perfect`. -/
@[stacks 09RK "erratum: the claim that j is a regular immersion is false as written; \
counterexample with Z = Spec k, Y = Spec k ⊔ Spec k, X = Spec k ⊔ Spec k[ε]/(ε²); the corrected \
statement is exists_opens_isRegularImmersion_comp_of_perfect"]
theorem not_forall_isRegularImmersion_of_perfect_of_isRegularImmersion_comp :
    ¬ ∀ (Z Y X : Scheme.{u}) (i : Z ⟶ Y) (j : Y ⟶ X) [IsImmersion i] [IsImmersion j],
        IsLocallyNoetherian X → AlgebraicGeometry.Perfect i →
          IsRegularImmersion (i ≫ j) → IsRegularImmersion i ∧ IsRegularImmersion j := by
  intro h
  exact not_isRegularImmersion_erratumOuter k
    (h _ _ _ (erratumInner k) (erratumOuter k) inferInstance (perfect_erratumInner k)
      (isRegularImmersion_erratumInner_comp k)).2

end Stacks.Examples

end
